of getting that one habit of a good workout in.
It doesn't have to be fitness. You talk to some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.720 --> 00:14:30.540
people, comedians for example or athletes, will&nbsp;
talk about how they have this visualization habit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.540 --> 00:14:35.700
before they step out on the stage. If they get&nbsp;
that in, they recite what a good performance is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.700 --> 00:14:40.740
going to look like, that helps them perform in&nbsp;
the moment. Or CEOs will talk about a meditation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.740 --> 00:14:45.060
habit, and if they get their 15 minutes of&nbsp;
meditation in in the morning, that sets them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.060 --> 00:14:48.720
up for the rest of the day being productive.
I think it just comes down to asking yourself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.440 --> 00:14:55.380
when I'm living a good day, when I'm on, what are&nbsp;
some of the key habits that are part of that? Then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.380 --> 00:15:00.120
maybe rather than worrying about everything and&nbsp;
trying to hit every little domino along the way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.120 --> 00:15:04.560
what's the lead domino? What's that&nbsp;
first action that's upstream from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.560 --> 00:15:08.880
other productive things? Can I just pour my&nbsp;
energy into making sure I do that today and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.880 --> 00:15:13.920
trust that the momentum will carry me forward?
Okay, so your question was, what are some other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:13.920 --> 00:15:18.960
examples of systems? It can take many different&nbsp;
forms. It could be a physical thing that helps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.960 --> 00:15:23.820
you be more productive. For example, this doesn't&nbsp;
work for everybody's job, but it works for mine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.820 --> 00:15:29.640
and I don't do it every day, but I probably do it&nbsp;
70 to or 80 percent of the time, which is I leave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.640 --> 00:15:35.280
my phone in another room until lunch each day.&nbsp;
Now, it gives me a chance, it gives me space,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.280 --> 00:15:41.580
to just work on my agenda rather than responding&nbsp;
to everybody else's agenda. I can actually show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.580 --> 00:15:46.980
up and have a few hours where I can do some&nbsp;
creative work. That's a system in a sense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.980 --> 00:15:51.060
it's a physical environment that promotes or&nbsp;
benefits the habit that I'm trying to build,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.060 --> 00:15:56.520
which is creative output or writing or whatever.
It could be digital things. For example, each week&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:56.520 --> 00:16:02.220
I write 3-2-1, this weekly newsletter. There are&nbsp;
three short ideas from me, two quotes from other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:02.220 --> 00:16:07.740
people and one question in each newsletter. I have&nbsp;
a spreadsheet that has three tabs, one of them is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:07.740 --> 00:16:14.160
ideas, one of them is quotes, and one of them is&nbsp;
questions. I've got hundreds of ideas that I've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.160 --> 00:16:19.200
generated or quotes that I've run across and read&nbsp;
or questions that I've spitballed and been, "Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.200 --> 00:16:23.760
maybe this could be in a newsletter sometime."&nbsp;
Because I have this system, which in this case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:23.760 --> 00:16:29.700
is just this spreadsheet of raw material, it only&nbsp;
takes me an hour or two to write the newsletter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:29.700 --> 00:16:35.340
because I have a lot to start from, I have good&nbsp;
starting material. The system of when I read a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:35.340 --> 00:16:40.440
book and come across a quote that I like, I type&nbsp;
it down and put it into the spreadsheet, that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:40.440 --> 00:16:43.920
a very simple system, but it makes the process&nbsp;
of writing the newsletter each week much easier.&nbsp;

00:16:44.940 --> 00:16:48.660
There are many different forms that could take,&nbsp;
it could be almost infinite in its variations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:48.660 --> 00:16:53.400
but it's any way that you set up the&nbsp;
environment, physical, digital or social,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.400 --> 00:17:00.540
to make the habit easier and more frictionless.
Tim Ferriss: Let's zoom in on the creative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.540 --> 00:17:06.360
process a little bit. We're going to talk&nbsp;
about also email lists, building email,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:06.360 --> 00:17:12.600
because I feel as though what was old is once&nbsp;
new again. Always, it's like every few years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.600 --> 00:17:18.420
I have to be like, "Guys, you need a way to&nbsp;
communicate with your audience that cannot be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:18.420 --> 00:17:20.867
taken away from you immediately by a platform."&nbsp;
I don't know how many times I've had to say this.&nbsp;

00:17:20.867 --> 00:17:24.221
James Clear: Yeah, email never dies,&nbsp;
but everybody talks about how it's dead.&nbsp;

00:17:24.221 --> 00:17:28.080
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, yeah. It's like the cat came&nbsp;
back the very next day, we thought it was a goner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:28.080 --> 00:17:33.840
but it wouldn't stay away. Email is not going&nbsp;
anywhere anytime immediately. We'll come back to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:33.840 --> 00:17:38.400
that, because it is a vehicle for transmitting&nbsp;
some of this creative work as you described.&nbsp;

00:17:39.600 --> 00:17:49.200
I have read a bit about your use, or past use,&nbsp;
of Asana for capturing ideas. I think this was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:49.200 --> 00:17:57.660
for asymmetry at the time, at least in the article&nbsp;
I read on every.to. Then I separately read about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:57.660 --> 00:18:04.800
a 600-page Google Doc that you had put together&nbsp;
and were compressing in various ways. Where have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:04.800 --> 00:18:12.540
you landed with idea capture, maybe web capture,&nbsp;
let's add that in because it may be a separate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:12.540 --> 00:18:17.940
category, what is your portfolio of tools?
James Clear: First of all, I don't think the tool&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:17.940 --> 00:18:24.480
matters that much. I think what does matter is&nbsp;
crafting great information flows and capturing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:24.480 --> 00:18:28.740
good ideas that you come across. Then depending on&nbsp;
what you're trying to do, in my case, I'm trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:28.740 --> 00:18:32.820
to create books or to write newsletters,&nbsp;
using that raw material that you collected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:32.820 --> 00:18:38.820
to create something great. I would say the big&nbsp;
picture game is always the same, which is first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:38.820 --> 00:18:45.600
I'm trying to craft better information flows.
Almost every idea that you have is downstream from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:45.600 --> 00:18:50.880
what you consume. We don't usually think about&nbsp;
it that way, but when you choose who to follow on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:50.880 --> 00:18:56.160
Twitter, you're choosing your future thoughts in a&nbsp;
sense, you're creating the information flow, what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:56.160 --> 00:19:00.900
the timeline, what the feed is going to look like.&nbsp;
Or when you choose what book to read or which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:00.900 --> 00:19:04.920
podcast episode to listen to, you're choosing the&nbsp;
thoughts that are going to arise. Now, you may not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:04.920 --> 00:19:10.620
necessarily know what they are, but over time you&nbsp;
can start to learn which sources of information&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:10.620 --> 00:19:17.160
are higher signal than others. I almost feel like&nbsp;
that is the main habit to try to build, especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:17.160 --> 00:19:21.790
in our current modern society, because information&nbsp;
is overflowing and so widely accessible.&nbsp;

00:19:21.790 --> 00:19:23.820
Tim Ferriss: So choosing your inputs.
James Clear: The person who creates better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:23.820 --> 00:19:27.240
information flows, gets better thoughts.
Tim Ferriss: How do you consume?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:27.840 --> 00:19:30.360
What do you choose?
James Clear: I've spent an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:30.360 --> 00:19:34.740
unreasonable amount of time curating my Twitter&nbsp;
feed. I think that makes me feel better about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:34.740 --> 00:19:40.320
spending time on it. I'm not necessarily saying&nbsp;
it's the best place, but because I have found a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:40.320 --> 00:19:45.120
really good set of people to follow I do get&nbsp;
useful ideas from Twitter. It's easy and I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:45.120 --> 00:19:48.180
probably spending more time on it than I should,&nbsp;
so at least now maybe it's a little more valuable.&nbsp;

00:19:48.180 --> 00:19:55.020
But the single highest thing for me is when&nbsp;
I find a book that is relevant to what I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:55.020 --> 00:20:00.480
writing about. If it's relevant to the topic,&nbsp;
and I mean that in a more granular fashion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:00.480 --> 00:20:05.280
let's say for Atomic Habits for example. I&nbsp;
don't mean a book about habits, I mean, hey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:05.280 --> 00:20:10.680
I'm writing a chapter on self-control and this&nbsp;
book has a story that relates to self-control,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:10.680 --> 00:20:16.740
or this book has a piece of research that relates&nbsp;
to that particular chapter, when I find the right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:16.740 --> 00:20:21.240
thing, it's hard for me to even get through a page&nbsp;
because I'm taking so many notes and it's sparking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:21.240 --> 00:20:25.187
so many ideas to write about. It'll take me an&nbsp;
entire morning just to get through three pages.&nbsp;

00:20:25.187 --> 00:20:31.380
Tim Ferriss: How do you find the gems that&nbsp;
are that dense? I have my own thoughts on how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:31.380 --> 00:20:36.000
I approach this, but there are a hundred&nbsp;
thousand plus books published a year in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:36.000 --> 00:20:41.640
the US in English alone, probably, something&nbsp;
along those lines. How do you ferret your way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:41.640 --> 00:20:45.960
through the maze to find some of those books?
James Clear: I think the first thing is you've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:45.960 --> 00:20:50.460
got to be willing to quit books fast. If&nbsp;
you have baggage around finishing books,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:50.460 --> 00:20:53.760
if you feel like you have to finish something&nbsp;
because you started it, then you're just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:53.760 --> 00:20:58.140
going to be stuck and you won't move on quickly&nbsp;
enough. There's this quote about Emerson, I think,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:58.140 --> 00:21:03.120
where it says something like, "He read like&nbsp;
a hawk looking for prey, scanning over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:03.120 --> 00:21:08.220
field." I think about that, I try to read&nbsp;
like that. I'm not reading for the enjoyment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:08.220 --> 00:21:12.660
or slowly unpacking a book the way that I would&nbsp;
with a sci-fi novel or something. It's like I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:12.660 --> 00:21:18.360
trying to find an insight. That's the first&nbsp;
thing, is start more books, quit most of them.&nbsp;

00:21:18.360 --> 00:21:24.240
The second thing is suggestions from friends are&nbsp;
always helpful. If you let people know, "Hey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:24.240 --> 00:21:28.500
I'm writing about this topic. What's the good book&nbsp;
that I should read?" I have found a couple great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:28.500 --> 00:21:34.260
ones that way, so just putting it out into the&nbsp;
universe. If you find a book that's good, go to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:34.260 --> 00:21:38.160
the back of the book and see what the references&nbsp;
are, and then go through those and just scan those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:38.160 --> 00:21:43.620
books on Amazon and a lot of the time you'll find&nbsp;
a couple other ones that are useful. If you find a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:43.620 --> 00:21:49.200
book that had something but you feel like maybe&nbsp;
it didn't quite hit it, go to Amazon and check&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:49.200 --> 00:21:53.340
all the three-star reviews for that book, or the&nbsp;
one-star reviews sometimes, and there'll be other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:53.340 --> 00:21:56.640
books recommended in there. They'll say, "Instead&nbsp;
of reading this, you should read this one."&nbsp;

00:21:56.640 --> 00:22:01.620
Tim Ferriss: The three-star reviews is&nbsp;
a key, because the five-star reviews are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:01.620 --> 00:22:06.600
just overly — not overly, I don't want to slam&nbsp;
five-star reviews, I love five-star reviews,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:06.600 --> 00:22:12.300
but they tend to not — I would say, for me at&nbsp;
least, one-star reviews are sometimes like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:12.300 --> 00:22:17.760
"I ordered a toaster and they sent me a book," and&nbsp;
you're like, "Aw, come on. You bought a book. Pay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:17.760 --> 00:22:18.827
attention to what you're clicking."
James Clear: Sure, yeah.&nbsp;

00:22:18.827 --> 00:22:23.520
Tim Ferriss: But the three-star reviews, the&nbsp;
most helpful or most critical three-star reviews,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:23.520 --> 00:22:30.120
you tend to get a lot of really helpful feedback.&nbsp;
I'll add too, for folks who might just be curious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:30.120 --> 00:22:34.260
because I read, for people who can see behind&nbsp;
me, I have bookshelves and many, many more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:34.260 --> 00:22:43.740
bookshelves, I'll very often go to Wikipedia and&nbsp;
Goodreads, I will see if the book is old enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:44.580 --> 00:22:49.080
and if it's iconic enough or has stood the test&nbsp;
of time, which is not true for all books that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:49.080 --> 00:22:54.720
I've read or do read, but there will often be a&nbsp;
Wikipedia page and there will almost always be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:55.560 --> 00:23:00.240
quotes from fill in the blank book, Goodreads,&nbsp;
if you search that on Google you'll get a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:00.240 --> 00:23:05.580
good selection. You can get a feel for, for&nbsp;
instance, Finite and Infinite Games by Carse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:05.580 --> 00:23:09.960
is that something you want to read? Well, you&nbsp;
could get a very good taste of it. It's like if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:09.960 --> 00:23:13.200
you don't like the trailer for the movie, you're&nbsp;
not going to like the movie. If you don't like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:13.200 --> 00:23:16.560
the Goodreads highlights, you're not going&nbsp;
to like the book, I can almost guarantee you.&nbsp;

00:23:17.340 --> 00:23:21.240
Side note on that, you were mentioning, what&nbsp;
am I optimizing for, which sounds very much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:21.780 --> 00:23:27.780
like it could be a Derek Sivers' question, he&nbsp;
has a lot of good questions. A variant on that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:27.780 --> 00:23:32.400
that I've also found helpful is, "What game am I&nbsp;
playing?" We're all playing games, so it's like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:33.000 --> 00:23:40.860
aside from shelter, food, a handful of the basics&nbsp;
at the very bottom of Maslow's Hierarchy of Needs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:40.860 --> 00:23:48.660
we're all playing games. What games are you&nbsp;
playing? What are you trying to win? Becoming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:48.660 --> 00:23:56.160
cognizant of that is, for me at least, step one.
James Clear: I think, I don't know if this is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:56.160 --> 00:24:00.356
question that you've asked before, but is that&nbsp;
game worth winning? Is this something that —&nbsp;

00:24:00.356 --> 00:24:01.560
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, absolutely.
James Clear: Am I climbing the right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:01.560 --> 00:24:05.400
mountain or do I need to be climbing a different&nbsp;
one? Because it's very important to make sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:05.400 --> 00:24:09.180
that you're in the right game.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, totally.&nbsp;

00:24:09.840 --> 00:24:14.280
You mentioned the tools not mattering so much,&nbsp;
choosing the inputs, we talked about books.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:16.080 --> 00:24:19.427
For capturing, when you're like,&nbsp;
"That's good, that's good, that's good."&nbsp;

00:24:19.427 --> 00:24:25.080
James Clear: You're right, I do use Asana mostly.&nbsp;
It's a very messy process and Asana's just a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:25.080 --> 00:24:29.880
holding ground for it, but I have the app on my&nbsp;
phone and Ive got it pulled up on my browser all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:29.880 --> 00:24:34.140
the time and so it's always right there. I don't&nbsp;
really care what the tool is, in this case this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:34.140 --> 00:24:38.280
is what I've been using for the last few years,&nbsp;
but it just needs to be something that is always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:38.280 --> 00:24:42.000
present and as soon as you have an idea, it&nbsp;
always goes into one place. You don't have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:42.000 --> 00:24:46.980
to think about where you're going to record it and&nbsp;
you don't end up with a random note on your iPhone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:46.980 --> 00:24:52.440
and another one in an Evernote doc and a third&nbsp;
thing in a Google Doc, and now it's hard to find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:52.440 --> 00:24:55.080
the ideas. If you have them all in one place —
Tim Ferriss: That sounds like the story of my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:55.080 --> 00:25:01.020
life right now, which is why I'm very interested&nbsp;
in your use of Asana, because I've used Asana to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:01.020 --> 00:25:09.540
I still use Asana for project management, task&nbsp;
management, but idea capture wasn't a use case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:09.540 --> 00:25:15.180
I'd really considered. Would you mind, just for&nbsp;
people listening, explaining? I know this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:16.020 --> 00:25:19.907
going to seem very pedestrian, but how&nbsp;
do you it without stuff getting lost?&nbsp;

00:25:19.907 --> 00:25:23.580
James Clear: Part of this is, again, I'm&nbsp;
trying to simplify things as much as possible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:23.580 --> 00:25:28.620
and we may talk about this more as we go through&nbsp;
the conversation, but I have a really small team,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:28.620 --> 00:25:33.540
it's me and one employee. I'm not interested in&nbsp;
having a large team and I want to be as effective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:33.540 --> 00:25:37.620
and as high leverage as possible. That means&nbsp;
that we need to think really carefully about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:37.620 --> 00:25:42.120
where we spend our time. If I always have a bunch&nbsp;
of different tools or I'm always switching between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:42.120 --> 00:25:46.500
different apps, that's just time that's wasted&nbsp;
if the job could be done in the same place.&nbsp;

00:25:47.820 --> 00:25:52.980
We use Asana to run the book launch or to run&nbsp;
the newsletter calendar or to for all the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:52.980 --> 00:25:56.940
stuff that we do throughout the day, and so I&nbsp;
was like, "Well, I can write it down here just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:56.940 --> 00:26:01.920
as well as I can write it down somewhere else."&nbsp;
Then once it gets categorized, it goes into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:01.920 --> 00:26:07.980
appropriate doc for that project. As an example,&nbsp;
let's say I'm reading a book and I come across an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:07.980 --> 00:26:13.860
idea that's relevant for the newsletter and&nbsp;
it's a quote. I'll probably label the task&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:13.860 --> 00:26:17.940
or the project of the new thing that I put into&nbsp;
Asana, I guess they usually call them tasks, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:17.940 --> 00:26:22.980
I label things based on the project it's going to.&nbsp;
I'll just say, "Quote," and then I'll paste the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:22.980 --> 00:26:28.080
quote in and then it's labeled. I'll go through&nbsp;
all those quotes, or Lindsay, my employee, will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:28.080 --> 00:26:31.080
go through all those quotes and we'll put them&nbsp;
into the spreadsheet so they're in the right tab.&nbsp;

00:26:31.080 --> 00:26:36.780
Or let's say the next thing I'm reading, I come&nbsp;
across an idea that sparks a thought for me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:36.780 --> 00:26:41.520
where I'm like, "Oh, that would fit into the next&nbsp;
chapter for the book." I'll write the idea out and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:41.520 --> 00:26:46.380
then I'll label it based on whatever the topic&nbsp;
of the book is. For Atomic Habits, the label&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:46.380 --> 00:26:51.420
might be Habits. At the beginning of any idea&nbsp;
I come across that would fit for the book, I'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:51.420 --> 00:26:57.480
put Habits. Then a lot of the time I'll actually&nbsp;
put a second category for the chapter. Let's say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:57.480 --> 00:27:04.020
that this particular idea was for the chapter&nbsp;
on self-control, it'll be Habits, Self-Control,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:04.020 --> 00:27:10.140
and then I'll type the idea out. Then a couple&nbsp;
times a week I'll go through everything that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:10.140 --> 00:27:13.440
labeled as Habits and then put it in the right&nbsp;
place in the doc and then it's all there.&nbsp;

00:27:13.440 --> 00:27:18.420
You could imagine one way to do this even faster,&nbsp;
which is you could just put it straight into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:18.420 --> 00:27:22.380
doc that it needs to live in. Instead of putting&nbsp;
it into Asana, I could put it straight into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:22.380 --> 00:27:26.340
3-2-1 Newsletter sheet or I could put it&nbsp;
straight into the chapter for the book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:26.340 --> 00:27:31.500
but I've noticed that the problem is I've got a&nbsp;
lot of projects going at one time and a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:31.500 --> 00:27:36.660
notes from places where things could live. If I'm&nbsp;
always having to remember which thing to pull up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:36.660 --> 00:27:41.460
then you either end up with a bazillion tabs&nbsp;
open or it just is hard to remember which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:41.460 --> 00:27:46.320
things need to go where at the exact moment&nbsp;
when you have the idea. A lot of the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:46.320 --> 00:27:51.300
I'll have an idea when I'm on a walk or in the&nbsp;
middle of a workout, and so I'm not in a position&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:51.300 --> 00:27:56.087
to pull up the book manuscript, I just need to be&nbsp;
able to get it down and then I can sort it later.&nbsp;

00:27:56.087 --> 00:27:59.940
Tim Ferriss: For people listening, what I'm&nbsp;
trying to do in this conversation, we'll see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:59.940 --> 00:28:04.680
how successful it is, is to zoom in and zoom out.&nbsp;
We'll zoom out and we'll talk about the conceptual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:04.680 --> 00:28:12.540
framework or a particular focal point, whether&nbsp;
it's within, say, the Atomic Habits paradigm or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:12.540 --> 00:28:20.580
outside of it, just given what you focus on James,&nbsp;
and then zoom in so we can take a look at how does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:20.580 --> 00:28:30.180
James run systems or develop systems in location X&nbsp;
in his life. The one-employee small team is where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:30.180 --> 00:28:33.240
I'd like to go next if that's okay with you.
James Clear: Sure, yep.&nbsp;

00:28:33.240 --> 00:28:43.380
Tim Ferriss: How do you, I suppose, try to build&nbsp;
systems, ensure maximal leverage, with a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:43.380 --> 00:28:48.060
small team? This is always of interest to me. I&nbsp;
have a small team, it has always been of interest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:48.060 --> 00:28:53.460
to me, like Small Giants by Bo Burlingham. I&nbsp;
think the subtitle is Companies That Choose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:53.460 --> 00:29:01.020
to Be Great Instead of Big or something like&nbsp;
that. I've always been fascinated by positive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:01.020 --> 00:29:05.580
constraints. One you could set is I'm not going&nbsp;
to have a big team, I'm not going to hire a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:05.580 --> 00:29:10.020
lot of people. Then you have to make decisions&nbsp;
that fall in line with not optimizing for that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:10.020 --> 00:29:11.687
but operating within that constraint.
James Clear: Sure.&nbsp;

00:29:11.687 --> 00:29:14.400
Tim Ferriss: What are some of&nbsp;
the productivity principles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:14.400 --> 00:29:19.860
guiding questions, and so on that you have?
James Clear: First, I'll just say I'm not saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:19.860 --> 00:29:24.480
this is how everybody should run their business,&nbsp;
it's just how I choose to run mine. You have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:24.480 --> 00:29:29.400
choose what you're trying to accomplish. In&nbsp;
my case, I don't want to have a big team,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:29.400 --> 00:29:35.700
but I do want to make as big of an impact as&nbsp;
possible. The first question I ask is rather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:35.700 --> 00:29:40.260
than optimizing for money, let's optimize for&nbsp;
time, so how do I want to spend my days? Then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:40.260 --> 00:29:44.640
I try to make as few choices as possible that&nbsp;
violate that answer. You've got to keep coming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:44.640 --> 00:29:50.760
back to that. How do I want to spend my days? What&nbsp;
do I want my time to be spent on? Within that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:50.760 --> 00:29:57.600
how can we reach the most people possible or make&nbsp;
the most money possible, but not without violating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:57.600 --> 00:30:01.020
that, because there's all kinds of things you do.&nbsp;
I could staff up, I can build a bigger business,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:01.020 --> 00:30:05.640
whatever, but I'm not interested in spending&nbsp;
my days like that. That's the first thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:05.640 --> 00:30:09.840
is you've got to decide what you're trying to do.
A question that I love and that I keep coming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:09.840 --> 00:30:13.860
back to for increasing leverage, and I try&nbsp;
to encourage both Lindsay and I to think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:13.860 --> 00:30:18.600
about this over and over again, is: what is the&nbsp;
work that keeps working for us once it's done?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:19.800 --> 00:30:25.860
As an example, when Atomic Habits came out, I did&nbsp;
a ton of interviews. Some of those interviews were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:25.860 --> 00:30:32.160
on radio, and I don't really do radio interviews&nbsp;
anymore because as soon as I get done with that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:32.160 --> 00:30:37.320
10-minute segment or whatever it was, all the&nbsp;
work that I put in it vanishes. Nobody's listening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:37.320 --> 00:30:42.600
anymore, it's off the air. Whereas a podcast,&nbsp;
what we're doing right now, is being recorded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:42.600 --> 00:30:48.420
and so people can continue to listen to it. I've&nbsp;
done a bunch of podcasts for the book and there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:48.420 --> 00:30:52.500
somebody somewhere listening to one of them right&nbsp;
now. It's almost like there are multiple versions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:52.500 --> 00:30:57.900
of James out there and they're all continuing&nbsp;
to work right now. That's an example of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:57.900 --> 00:31:03.900
work that keeps working for you once it's done.
If you can do one or two things a day that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:03.900 --> 00:31:08.640
going to keep working for you in the long&nbsp;
run, man, you end up two or three or five&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:08.640 --> 00:31:13.260
years later and you just have this tidal wave&nbsp;
of previous effort that is working for you.&nbsp;

00:31:13.260 --> 00:31:17.460
So let me give you a couple different examples&nbsp;
and stories from my personal experience.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:18.300 --> 00:31:22.320
I wrote this article a couple years ago, it&nbsp;
was called "The Physics of Productivity," and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:22.320 --> 00:31:26.880
it was just taking like Newton's three laws and&nbsp;
applying them to productivity in kind of this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:26.880 --> 00:31:32.880
I don't know, hopefully clever way. And it did&nbsp;
fine. The article didn't blow up or anything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:32.880 --> 00:31:36.540
but it was just kind of a normal piece. And&nbsp;
it sat there on the site and people would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:36.540 --> 00:31:40.860
occasionally read it. And then a couple years&nbsp;
later, this journalist from the New York Times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:40.860 --> 00:31:45.660
read that article and they were writing this&nbsp;
piece and they linked to the original article&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:45.660 --> 00:31:49.260
in it. And it wasn't much, it was just like&nbsp;
a sentence, but they linked to the website.&nbsp;

00:31:49.860 --> 00:31:55.080
Well, there was this producer for CBS that&nbsp;
read the article in the New York Times and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:55.080 --> 00:31:58.560
then clicked through to my site and sent me an&nbsp;
email and said, "Hey, do you want to come on CBS&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:58.560 --> 00:32:03.960
This Morning and talk about this piece?" And so&nbsp;
I was like, "Okay, sure." So they flew me to New&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:03.960 --> 00:32:07.680
York and I did the segment and it was my first&nbsp;
time on TV and so I was all nervous about it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:07.680 --> 00:32:14.040
but I tried to do my best and have a good segment.&nbsp;
And as soon as it got done, I went over to Gayle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:14.040 --> 00:32:18.840
King and I said, "Hey, I have this book coming&nbsp;
out in 10 months." So this was 10 months before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:18.840 --> 00:32:23.848
Atomic Habits. "I would love to come back and do&nbsp;
a segment about that when the book comes out."&nbsp;

00:32:23.848 --> 00:32:25.020
Tim Ferriss: Smart.
James Clear: And she said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:25.020 --> 00:32:28.620
"Sure thing. We'll have you back. Just make&nbsp;
sure that we're your first stop. Don't do any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:28.620 --> 00:32:34.620
other interviews on TV shows before us." And so&nbsp;
as soon as I left, I got her email and I sent a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:34.620 --> 00:32:39.780
message to her and the publisher and everybody and&nbsp;
I got on the calendar for launch day. And that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:39.780 --> 00:32:45.600
how I got on CBS This Morning for the launch of&nbsp;
Atomic Habits. And that ended up being, I think,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:45.600 --> 00:32:51.540
a kind of an important moment for the launch of&nbsp;
the book because it made it feel like a thing.&nbsp;

00:32:52.140 --> 00:32:57.120
It wasn't just a guy launching a book, it&nbsp;
was like, oh, this is a bigger event. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:57.120 --> 00:33:01.440
I don't know how many books that segment&nbsp;
sold. I've tried to calculate it. Probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:02.160 --> 00:33:06.240
more than a thousand, probably less than&nbsp;
10,000. Probably somewhere in that range.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:07.920 --> 00:33:13.980
But as soon as the segment got over, they put&nbsp;
that clip on YouTube and then two hours later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:13.980 --> 00:33:18.360
we took the YouTube clip and emailed it out to&nbsp;
my audience and that was the launch email for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:18.360 --> 00:33:23.100
the book that day was like, "Here's my segment on&nbsp;
CBS This Morning. The book is out." And so what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:23.100 --> 00:33:28.080
I'm getting at is that work of writing that&nbsp;
article about the physics of productivity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:28.080 --> 00:33:33.720
that work continued to work for me in really&nbsp;
big ways three or five or six years later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:33.720 --> 00:33:38.100
even though the initial article didn't seem&nbsp;
like that big of a deal. And the reason it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:38.100 --> 00:33:42.300
worked out is because that was work that&nbsp;
kept working for me once it was done.&nbsp;

00:33:42.300 --> 00:33:50.460
So anytime you create an asset that can compound —&nbsp;
a blog post, a podcast episode, even simple stuff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:50.460 --> 00:33:54.720
like Twitter posts and Instagram posts are less&nbsp;
likely because they kind of have a short half&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:54.720 --> 00:34:00.240
life, but even those can be examples of putting&nbsp;
work out there that is still being recorded and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:00.240 --> 00:34:06.300
still being stumbled across by people online and&nbsp;
you're creating a large surface area for good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:06.300 --> 00:34:11.640
things to kind of break your way If you spend time&nbsp;
doing that. Now I think you can even do better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:11.640 --> 00:34:17.280
than that, which is not only can you create assets&nbsp;
that compound and keep working for you, you can do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:17.280 --> 00:34:25.260
it in a way where they layer on top of each other.
So I'll spend 20 minutes on a tweet and that seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:25.260 --> 00:34:31.200
kind of an excessive amount of time to spend&nbsp;
writing two sentences. But the time that I put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:31.200 --> 00:34:36.420
into that, I'll put it out there and it's its own&nbsp;
little asset, and then maybe if it does well, I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:36.420 --> 00:34:40.440
like, hey, we should use that in the newsletter.&nbsp;
And so it goes in the newsletter. And then maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:40.440 --> 00:34:45.000
it becomes the seed of an idea for a longer&nbsp;
chapter or an article in a book or something,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:45.000 --> 00:34:51.960
or it gets added to related ideas that can become&nbsp;
something bigger. And so now that 20 minutes is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:51.960 --> 00:34:57.360
actually doing a lot more work than just gaining&nbsp;
a few Twitter followers. And if you can find ways&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:57.360 --> 00:35:03.540
to layer those little assets on top of each other,&nbsp;
then you get three or five or six years down the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:03.540 --> 00:35:09.240
line and you really have dramatic effect. So those&nbsp;
are a couple different ways to try to operate in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:09.240 --> 00:35:14.700
a high leverage way with a really small team.
Tim Ferriss: In the last week, we're recording&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:14.700 --> 00:35:22.740
this towards the end of December, 2022, Twitter&nbsp;
is one example, has made a number of changes that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:22.740 --> 00:35:32.520
have suspended a number of my Twitter accounts.&nbsp;
And there's no explanation provided, no particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:33.600 --> 00:35:38.520
bad behavior that I or my team can identify,&nbsp;
but suspended nonetheless. And then they get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:38.520 --> 00:35:43.320
reinstated and then two days later they'll get&nbsp;
suspended again. Can't figure it out. But what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:43.320 --> 00:35:49.860
this means is much when, say, Facebook started,&nbsp;
I don't want to say requiring, highly encouraging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:49.860 --> 00:35:57.180
boosting, AKA paying for promotion of posts&nbsp;
to reach your previously organic audience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:58.140 --> 00:36:05.940
people's business models and audience size started&nbsp;
to effectively disintegrate. And having email is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:05.940 --> 00:36:10.020
so, so valuable, which is why I'm also spending&nbsp;
some time on this and it's important to me. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:10.800 --> 00:36:17.040
that's just a background, setting the table so&nbsp;
that I can ask you when something is going wrong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:17.040 --> 00:36:22.620
aside from reaching out to say your email service&nbsp;
provider and saying, "Hey, is something broken? Is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:22.620 --> 00:36:24.827
there a monkey wrench in the works?"
James Clear: Yeah.&nbsp;

00:36:24.827 --> 00:36:27.000
Tim Ferriss: How would you&nbsp;
go about figuring things out?&nbsp;

00:36:27.000 --> 00:36:33.300
James Clear: I like to think about it as there's&nbsp;
internal distribution. So those are the things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:33.300 --> 00:36:38.460
that I own. And you really can only own kind of&nbsp;
a few assets. You can own your website, you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:38.460 --> 00:36:44.280
own your email list, you can own your podcast.&nbsp;
And that's kind of roughly about it. And then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:44.280 --> 00:36:51.060
the other bucket is external. So this is other&nbsp;
people's platforms that you can have distribution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:51.060 --> 00:36:59.760
on, YouTube, TikTok, Twitter, Facebook, et&nbsp;
cetera. And as much as possible, I think the web,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:59.760 --> 00:37:05.160
it is very platform centric right now and people&nbsp;
spend a lot of time on those platforms. I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:05.160 --> 00:37:09.360
want to be on all of them because I don't have&nbsp;
enough time and energy to be good at all of them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:09.360 --> 00:37:15.000
but there are probably a few that are a good&nbsp;
fit for my audience. So Twitter and Instagram&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:15.000 --> 00:37:18.947
are the two that we've decided to focus on.
Tim Ferriss: No TikTok dances coming soon?&nbsp;

00:37:18.947 --> 00:37:24.600
James Clear: No, no TikTok routines yet. If I&nbsp;
figure out how to tie that into habits, we'll see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:24.600 --> 00:37:33.120
what comes. So I post on those two platforms and&nbsp;
then I try to build an audience there. But also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:33.120 --> 00:37:37.800
you'll notice a lot of the posts that I put up&nbsp;
on there, so Instagram for example, I have a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:37.800 --> 00:37:41.760
weird Instagram account because I don't share any&nbsp;
pictures of myself. I'm not really interested in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:41.760 --> 00:37:46.920
me as an individual being out there, but it's all&nbsp;
about the ideas. And so it's just a bunch of text,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:47.820 --> 00:37:54.240
but on many of them, at the bottom of the image&nbsp;
or in the caption, it'll say, here's a quote.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:54.240 --> 00:37:58.920
And then at the bottom it'll say, get more ideas&nbsp;
like this, sign up for 3-2-1 and then it'll have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:58.920 --> 00:38:05.940
a link to the newsletter. And so the idea is to&nbsp;
subtly be encouraging people to go off platform&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:05.940 --> 00:38:11.820
and join one of the assets or one of the pieces of&nbsp;
audience that I do own, one of the internal ones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:11.820 --> 00:38:16.140
like the website or the newsletter or whatever.
And so I try to get those things to work together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:16.140 --> 00:38:22.080
and cross link between the two, and then they can&nbsp;
start to build each other. And I do it the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:22.080 --> 00:38:27.600
way as well, like when I send out a newsletter,&nbsp;
sometimes if the ideas are short enough and they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:27.600 --> 00:38:31.980
would make a good tweet, I'll include a little&nbsp;
link under each idea, it'll just say, click to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:31.980 --> 00:38:37.740
tweet. And so now the newsletter is driving action&nbsp;
on Twitter. And so it all kind of like you create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:37.740 --> 00:38:42.480
this little spider web, this kind of ecosystem&nbsp;
where everything is kind of driving each other and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:43.860 --> 00:38:50.700
it all kind of grows together. But the&nbsp;
ultimate idea is to try to point back the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:50.700 --> 00:38:55.440
external platforms to the distribution&nbsp;
sources that you do own so that you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:55.440 --> 00:39:00.047
not beholden to any particular one when they&nbsp;
decide to change their strategy or approach,&nbsp;

00:39:00.047 --> 00:39:05.100
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. Which is specifically why&nbsp;
a whole bunch of stuff is getting banned or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:05.100 --> 00:39:08.700
shadow banned right now on Twitter. And&nbsp;
so the best theory I could come up with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:08.700 --> 00:39:15.660
and I can't get this verified or denied, is that&nbsp;
because some of these accounts which were created&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:15.660 --> 00:39:22.980
for a new podcast that came out have legitimate&nbsp;
contact, but they all link out to Apple Podcasts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:22.980 --> 00:39:30.300
That's the only plausible explanation that we&nbsp;
can come up with for why they have been locked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:30.300 --> 00:39:35.520
and they keep getting unlocked. So I don't want&nbsp;
to make anybody seem like the bad guy here, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:35.520 --> 00:39:42.720
it highlights the fragility of having something&nbsp;
that is entirely dependent on a platform that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:42.720 --> 00:39:46.380
don't own. And also if you were to read your terms&nbsp;
of service, you're probably giving all of that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:46.380 --> 00:39:52.740
content to the platform as well on some level.
And I recall chatting with this guy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:52.740 --> 00:39:58.020
this is many years ago, before the organic&nbsp;
reach was throttled a lot on Facebook,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:00.420 --> 00:40:05.580
chatting with a guy who had a multi-million&nbsp;
dollar business built on a few Facebook pages&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:05.580 --> 00:40:13.560
and massively successful. And I asked him what it&nbsp;
felt like to run those businesses. And he said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:13.560 --> 00:40:17.940
"I feel like I have the most profitable&nbsp;
McDonald's in the world built on top of an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:17.940 --> 00:40:26.369
active volcano." It could go away at any minute,&nbsp;
and I don't want to live with that anxiety also.&nbsp;

00:40:26.369 --> 00:40:27.600
James Clear: Sure.
Tim Ferriss: And just to add a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:27.600 --> 00:40:33.660
few things just to going to feather them&nbsp;
in with your comments. One is it's very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:33.660 --> 00:40:41.520
important to adapt. I would add something to&nbsp;
that, which is it's very important to adapt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:41.520 --> 00:40:49.680
but I think it's really critical to have a good&nbsp;
explanation for how you are adapting and not to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:49.680 --> 00:40:55.560
discard for the sake of discarding. And just as an&nbsp;
example, to give people a window into my thinking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:55.560 --> 00:41:01.080
Reddit and some of these other platforms still can&nbsp;
drive a lot of traffic, but they're not sexy and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:01.080 --> 00:41:06.720
people have started to neglect them. So there's&nbsp;
far less competition on some of these older,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:06.720 --> 00:41:11.640
let's just call it platforms that are actually&nbsp;
still innovating quite well in the case of Reddit.&nbsp;

00:41:12.360 --> 00:41:18.060
So I've thought about revisiting some&nbsp;
of the vintage powerhouses because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:18.900 --> 00:41:26.340
there's been such a mass migration to particularly&nbsp;
short form video. It just seems like a bloodbath.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:27.120 --> 00:41:34.740
And I would also say, something I think you've&nbsp;
done very well is set constraints on what you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:34.740 --> 00:41:40.380
will or will not do, say on social platforms.&nbsp;
Because one of the risks of adapting is that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:40.380 --> 00:41:49.920
become a slave to the audience in such a way that&nbsp;
you morph into this funhouse mirror caricature of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:49.920 --> 00:41:55.080
yourself. And I've seen this happen to a lot of&nbsp;
people where they put out 10 posts and the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:55.080 --> 00:42:03.240
extreme of those gets the most engagement or&nbsp;
feedback or positive likes, et cetera, and so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:03.240 --> 00:42:09.660
they then put out five more like that, the most&nbsp;
extreme gets the best response. And a year later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:10.440 --> 00:42:19.260
not only has their social feed become like&nbsp;
the craziest infomercial-pitchman-yelling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:19.800 --> 00:42:29.460
circus, but this is the greater risk is that&nbsp;
they in real life have started to become the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:29.460 --> 00:42:34.380
mask that they've created for themselves.
This is a very real risk. So setting up the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:34.380 --> 00:42:39.480
constraints on what you will or will not do in&nbsp;
advance before you get on that playing field,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:39.480 --> 00:42:44.460
I think is really, really, at least for&nbsp;
me, very, very important. How do you think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:44.460 --> 00:42:48.600
about that? Or have you thought about that?
James Clear: Well, this connects to one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:48.600 --> 00:42:54.420
the core ideas in Atomic Habits, which is your&nbsp;
habits are how you embody a particular identity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:54.420 --> 00:43:00.780
So the aspects, the behaviors that you perform&nbsp;
each day are reinforcing or shaping the story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:00.780 --> 00:43:04.920
that you have about yourself. I think it's&nbsp;
important to ask yourself: who is the type&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:04.920 --> 00:43:10.500
of person I wish to become? What's the type of&nbsp;
identity I want to be reinforcing? And your habits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:10.500 --> 00:43:15.600
are not the only things that influence that in&nbsp;
life. Every experience is part of who you are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:15.600 --> 00:43:22.980
but by virtue of the fact that they get repeated&nbsp;
again and again, they have an outsized influence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:22.980 --> 00:43:28.200
on your story. And so the phrase I like to keep&nbsp;
in mind is: every action you take is a vote for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:28.200 --> 00:43:30.583
the type of person you wish to become.
Tim Ferriss: That's a great line.&nbsp;

00:43:30.583 --> 00:43:36.840
James Clear: And so as you show up each day and&nbsp;
make another YouTube video or do another TikTok&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:36.840 --> 00:43:41.820
video, you're casting a vote for being that kind&nbsp;
of person. And if you let the algorithm run it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:41.820 --> 00:43:47.220
