00:58:41.520 --> 00:58:46.560
or interesting or different. And so you do those&nbsp;
and then you turn around six months later and you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:46.560 --> 00:58:51.660
don't have any time to do the thing that made you&nbsp;
successful in the first place. So yeah, you've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:51.660 --> 00:58:56.160
got to be careful about what you say yes to.
Tim Ferriss: So I'd like to ask you about two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:57.000 --> 00:59:04.920
goals / habits / behavioral modifications that&nbsp;
are of interest to me personally, and also I would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:04.920 --> 00:59:09.420
imagine of interest to other people listening.&nbsp;
And I'm curious because you've had 10 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:09.420 --> 00:59:14.700
copies sold, you've interacted with or certainly&nbsp;
observed a lot of your readers, people who have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:14.700 --> 00:59:19.080
tested different things from the book. Let's&nbsp;
start with, well, the two that come to mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:19.080 --> 00:59:26.160
and I've actually, I'm good at the second, but the&nbsp;
two are, cutting back on caffeine and meditating.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:26.700 --> 00:59:31.800
So the meditating is an addition. The cutting back&nbsp;
on caffeine, a lot of people would think of as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:31.800 --> 00:59:38.280
subtraction. I would love to hear any thoughts&nbsp;
you might have on cutting back on caffeine.&nbsp;

00:59:38.280 --> 00:59:43.800
James Clear: Sure. So if you want to&nbsp;
break a bad habit, there are three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:43.800 --> 00:59:48.720
potential paths you could take. So the first is&nbsp;
you can eliminate it entirely. So elimination.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:49.560 --> 00:59:54.900
Second option is you can reduce it. So you could&nbsp;
curtail it to the desired degree. I don't want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:54.900 --> 00:59:59.340
never drink caffeine, I just want to drink less of&nbsp;
it. And the third option is you could substitute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:59.340 --> 01:00:06.060
it, you could replace it with a different habit.&nbsp;
And of those three, oftentimes replacing it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:06.060 --> 01:00:11.820
is actually the more effective option. So for&nbsp;
example, if you get your caffeine from drinking&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:11.820 --> 01:00:16.980
Coke or soda or something like that, then maybe&nbsp;
you find out, hey, you know something I really&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:16.980 --> 01:00:21.720
love from this experience is I actually just like&nbsp;
drinking a carbonated beverage. And so it's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:21.720 --> 01:00:26.940
carbonation that I like. And maybe if I substitute&nbsp;
it with sparkling water or something like that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:26.940 --> 01:00:32.880
I still get the carbonation sensation, but I don't&nbsp;
have the caffeine associated with it anymore.&nbsp;

01:00:32.880 --> 01:00:38.820
And so that's a way of substituting for that&nbsp;
behavior. And you still get something that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:38.820 --> 01:00:44.880
experience provides. This is actually kind of an&nbsp;
important just larger picture, big picture thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:44.880 --> 01:00:52.020
about habits, which is every habit that you have,&nbsp;
we build habits to solve the repeated problems&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:52.020 --> 01:00:57.720
that we face in life. And I'm using problems in a&nbsp;
very general sense here. Let's say that you come&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:57.720 --> 01:01:04.020
home from work and you feel tired and exhausted&nbsp;
from a long day. Well, in a sense, coming home&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:04.020 --> 01:01:10.080
from work at 6:00 p.m. and feeling tired is a&nbsp;
problem. And especially if you experience it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:10.080 --> 01:01:15.420
repeatedly, you've got to come up with some kind&nbsp;
of solution for that. And generally speaking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:15.420 --> 01:01:21.840
we just try things out in life. And so you can&nbsp;
imagine one person solves the problem of feeling&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:21.840 --> 01:01:27.000
exhausted by scrolling Instagram mindlessly&nbsp;
for 30 minutes. And another person solves&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:27.000 --> 01:01:31.740
that problem by playing video games for an hour.
And a third person solves it by going for a run&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:31.740 --> 01:01:38.820
for 20 minutes. And those are all solutions to the&nbsp;
same kind of underlying problem, but some of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:38.820 --> 01:01:44.760
are more healthy or more productive or service&nbsp;
better than others. And what do you think the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:44.760 --> 01:01:51.420
odds are that the solutions you've come up with to&nbsp;
the repeated problems in your life are the optimal&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:51.420 --> 01:01:57.480
one? It's just so unlikely that whatever you&nbsp;
happen to have stumbled into throughout life is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:57.480 --> 01:02:02.400
the perfect way or the best habit that serves you&nbsp;
most. So I think what I'm trying to get out there&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:02.400 --> 01:02:06.000
is maybe take a little bit of the pressure off&nbsp;
yourself, and don't worry about judging yourself&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:06.000 --> 01:02:11.220
so much. You're just trying to solve the repeated&nbsp;
problems that you face. But once you realize that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:11.220 --> 01:02:16.320
it's unlikely that your current solutions&nbsp;
are the optimal solutions, well now maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:16.320 --> 01:02:20.580
we can step outside and above ourselves and look&nbsp;
down and try to come up with a better solution.&nbsp;

01:02:20.580 --> 01:02:25.080
So rather than drinking a Coke to get the&nbsp;
carbonation, we can drink sparkling water.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:25.080 --> 01:02:30.480
So that's one example for the substitution. If&nbsp;
we want to take the other path of reduction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:30.480 --> 01:02:37.620
something I've noticed about myself is if I get a&nbsp;
six pack of beer and I put it in the front of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:37.620 --> 01:02:42.180
fridge, like in the door, or on a shelf that's&nbsp;
like right at eye level, I'll drink one every&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:42.180 --> 01:02:46.260
night just because it's there. But if I take it&nbsp;
and put it on the lowest shelf in the fridge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:46.260 --> 01:02:49.800
and it's kind of all the way back in the corner,&nbsp;
I can't really see it unless I'm bending down.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:50.460 --> 01:02:54.420
It'll sit there for two weeks or three&nbsp;
weeks. And so I'm like, did I want it or&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:54.420 --> 01:03:00.540
not? If it was obvious, then I grab one but&nbsp;
if it wasn't, then I avoid it. And I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:00.540 --> 01:03:04.140
that's a simple question to ask yourself.
Where do I get my caffeine? Is it coffee,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:04.140 --> 01:03:10.560
is it soda? And is that really obvious? Is it like&nbsp;
the coffee sitting right out on the counter? Is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:10.560 --> 01:03:16.080
the soda the first thing I see at eye level when&nbsp;
I open the fridge? And how can I make it less&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:16.080 --> 01:03:21.600
obvious? In Atomic Habits, there are kind of what&nbsp;
I call the four laws of behavior change. And it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:21.600 --> 01:03:26.340
just this big picture view of how to build a good&nbsp;
habit. And so if you want to build a good habit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:26.340 --> 01:03:31.800
you want to make your habits obvious, attractive,&nbsp;
easy, and satisfying. And if you want to break a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:31.800 --> 01:03:35.160
bad habit, you just do the opposite of those&nbsp;
four. So you want to make it invisible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:35.160 --> 01:03:39.360
make it unattractive, make it difficult,&nbsp;
and make it unsatisfying. And so I think,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:39.360 --> 01:03:44.220
how can you make coffee or soda invisible?
How can you make it difficult to drink? Keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:44.220 --> 01:03:49.500
it outside of the house, et cetera. So there are&nbsp;
a variety of strategies you could use here. Let's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:49.500 --> 01:03:54.600
say you live with a family and other people are&nbsp;
going to want to drink it, but you don't want to.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:54.600 --> 01:03:59.640
Well, there's this little kind of diabolical&nbsp;
Tupperware container called the Kitchen Safe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:59.640 --> 01:04:06.840
and it has a lockbox code on the top of it. And&nbsp;
so you could give the other family members the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:06.840 --> 01:04:12.000
lockbox code, but then they don't give it to you&nbsp;
and you just put the soda inside of that and so it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:12.000 --> 01:04:17.160
stays in that Tupperware and you can't access it.&nbsp;
So finding ways to increase friction or to make it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:17.160 --> 01:04:21.540
difficult or to make it less obvious,&nbsp;
those are all ways to potentially curtail&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:22.200 --> 01:04:24.360
the caffeine habit.
Tim Ferriss: The&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:24.360 --> 01:04:27.707
locking Tupperware, what is it called again?
