01:05:47.340 --> 01:05:53.580
Let's talk about positive. And you already&nbsp;
alluded to the four principles or laws or&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:53.580 --> 01:05:59.880
ingredients. But meditating, I think for&nbsp;
a lot of folks, meditating is in the air.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:00.540 --> 01:06:07.860
They've heard about it on this podcast. "I know I&nbsp;
should do it, I just don't. By the time I have my&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:07.860 --> 01:06:14.880
coffee and do this and make the sandwiches for&nbsp;
the kids, it's too late." How would you suggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:14.880 --> 01:06:20.460
people approach, say meditating in the morning?
James Clear: So couple things to think about&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:20.460 --> 01:06:24.480
here. So again, let me just run through the four&nbsp;
laws real quick just as a primer for this answer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:24.480 --> 01:06:30.420
So if you want to build a good habit, roughly&nbsp;
speaking, there are four things that you can do.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:30.420 --> 01:06:35.940
You want to make it obvious, you want to make it&nbsp;
attractive, you want to make it easy and you want&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:35.940 --> 01:06:41.400
to make it satisfying. So obvious, attractive,&nbsp;
easy, satisfying. Now if you're sitting there&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:41.400 --> 01:06:45.060
and you're thinking, how can I get myself to&nbsp;
meditate more? You can just turn those into&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:45.060 --> 01:06:49.680
questions and you can say, how can I make the&nbsp;
behavior more obvious? How can I make it more&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:49.680 --> 01:06:54.000
attractive? How can I make it easier? How can&nbsp;
I make it more satisfying? And you'll start to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:54.000 --> 01:06:58.920
notice different things that you could do. So for&nbsp;
example, how can I make meditation more obvious?&nbsp;

01:06:58.920 --> 01:07:03.960
Well, do you have a clear space where you're going&nbsp;
to do this? Maybe you need a meditation pillow and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:03.960 --> 01:07:08.340
it's in the corner of your bedroom or it's in the&nbsp;
corner of some other room that is the dedicated&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:08.340 --> 01:07:12.720
meditation space. And this is exactly where it&nbsp;
happens. So it's obvious where the behavior's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:12.720 --> 01:07:17.700
going to occur. Make it attractive. There are&nbsp;
many different types of meditation and there&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:17.700 --> 01:07:22.740
are a lot of ways to get into it. And this is&nbsp;
true for any habit, by the way. For some reason,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:22.740 --> 01:07:28.620
I think we often choose habits that we feel&nbsp;
like we should do, but it's not necessarily&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:28.620 --> 01:07:36.060
the one that we want to do individually. And&nbsp;
there may not necessarily be a thousand ways&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:36.060 --> 01:07:40.980
to do everything in life, but there's almost&nbsp;
always more than one way. And you should choose&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:40.980 --> 01:07:46.320
the version that you're most genuinely excited&nbsp;
about. That is most appealing and interesting to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:46.320 --> 01:07:52.980
you. Because if you're genuinely interested in it,&nbsp;
there's going to be all kinds of ways to improve.&nbsp;

01:07:52.980 --> 01:07:57.600
You'll find all sorts of things that you could&nbsp;
refine or make it better. But if you're not&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:57.600 --> 01:08:02.220
actually interested, if you're not genuinely&nbsp;
engaged in the task, even the obvious stuff is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:02.220 --> 01:08:06.660
going to feel like a hassle. It is going to feel&nbsp;
like a chore even if it's straightforward. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:07.320 --> 01:08:10.860
do you want a guided meditation? Maybe it'd&nbsp;
be nice to have somebody walk you through it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:10.860 --> 01:08:17.580
Or do you want to find a meditation that has&nbsp;
lovely music associated with it? Do you not&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:17.580 --> 01:08:20.700
want anything? Do you just want silence? And&nbsp;
you want to be able to hear yourself think&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:20.700 --> 01:08:24.780
for a minute or listen to your own breath for&nbsp;
five minutes? And that's kind of the objective.&nbsp;

01:08:24.780 --> 01:08:29.760
But what sounds most attractive and appealing&nbsp;
to you? Try to find a version of the habit that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:29.760 --> 01:08:34.980
you're actually interested in. This actually,&nbsp;
I think connects to the timing piece that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:34.980 --> 01:08:39.180
were talking about, Tim, which is yeah, in the&nbsp;
morning is a great time for a lot of people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:39.180 --> 01:08:43.320
but if you have young kids and your four-year-old&nbsp;
is running around and you're trying to figure out&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:43.320 --> 01:08:47.700
how to get pants on them and you need to make&nbsp;
breakfast, that's probably not a good time to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:47.700 --> 01:08:52.860
do it. So find a time and a space where that&nbsp;
habit can live, where it's attractive and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:52.860 --> 01:08:56.040
you're not just going to end up frustrated&nbsp;
because you're trying to swim upstream.&nbsp;

01:08:56.040 --> 01:09:03.060
Make it easy. So rather than doing 15 or 20&nbsp;
minutes or 30 minutes of meditation, which hey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:03.060 --> 01:09:08.160
that sounds great because your favorite guru&nbsp;
does it. But listen, why not just do 60 seconds?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:08.160 --> 01:09:13.080
Because if you can master the art of showing up,&nbsp;
if you can just do it for a minute and actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:13.080 --> 01:09:17.400
stick to that day in and day out, then you're&nbsp;
starting to build the habit and now you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:17.400 --> 01:09:22.320
something, you've like gained a foothold and you&nbsp;
can advance the next level. One of the things I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:22.320 --> 01:09:26.160
recommend in the book is called the two-minute&nbsp;
rule. And it says, just take whatever habit&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:26.160 --> 01:09:30.180
you're trying to build and you scale it down to&nbsp;
something that takes two minutes or less to do.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:30.180 --> 01:09:37.440
So read 30 books a year becomes read one page&nbsp;
or meditate five days a week for 30 minutes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:37.440 --> 01:09:43.560
becomes meditate for 60 seconds and you're just&nbsp;
trying to master the art of showing up. A habit&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:43.560 --> 01:09:49.080
must be established before it can be improved.&nbsp;
It's got to become the standard before you worry&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:49.080 --> 01:09:54.300
about optimizing it into some perfect thing.
So make it easy to do. Make it easy to show up.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:54.300 --> 01:09:59.100
And then the final thing is make it satisfying.&nbsp;
Now, if you've done those first three steps well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:59.100 --> 01:10:03.180
it's obvious, it's an attractive version of it.&nbsp;
It's pretty easy to do. You're probably going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:03.180 --> 01:10:06.540
feel pretty good about yourself because you're at&nbsp;
the end of the meditation session now. So that'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:06.540 --> 01:10:12.780
probably be pretty satisfying. But you can layer&nbsp;
on some additional benefit. Maybe you get to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:12.780 --> 01:10:17.700
your favorite type of coffee or your favorite&nbsp;
drink after that, or maybe you get to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:18.720 --> 01:10:24.840
a bubble bath or a walk in the woods or whatever&nbsp;
sounds motivating to you. So find some way to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:24.840 --> 01:10:30.120
add some additional positive emotions to the&nbsp;
experience because if you feel good about it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:30.120 --> 01:10:34.860
you're going to want to repeat it. And this is&nbsp;
something that in Atomic Habits, I call it the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:34.860 --> 01:10:40.320
cardinal rule of behavior change, which is&nbsp;
behaviors that get rewarded, get repeated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:40.320 --> 01:10:45.960
and behaviors that get punished, get avoided.
And it's so basic, it's so obvious. But all&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:45.960 --> 01:10:51.720
humans want to feel good. We all want to&nbsp;
have positive emotions, to be supported,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:51.720 --> 01:10:58.140
to be loved, to be rewarded, to have something&nbsp;
that feels good. And so how can you get that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:58.140 --> 01:11:03.120
feeling and associate it with your habits?&nbsp;
That's kind of the core idea. And ultimately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:03.120 --> 01:11:08.580
this connects back to what we talked about with&nbsp;
identity, which is the perfect version is when&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:08.580 --> 01:11:14.880
you perform a habit and you feel good because it's&nbsp;
reinforcing your desired identity. I'm the type of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:14.880 --> 01:11:19.260
person who wants to meditate each day, or I'm&nbsp;
the type of person who doesn't miss meditation&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:19.260 --> 01:11:24.900
sessions. And then even if it's only 60 seconds,&nbsp;
you can feel good about doing it because it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:24.900 --> 01:11:30.467
reinforcing your desired identity. So that's kind&nbsp;
of a quick case study on how to apply the ideas.&nbsp;

01:11:30.467 --> 01:11:34.080
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, totally. And as you're&nbsp;
speaking, I'm thinking about all these other&nbsp;&nbsp;

