01:12:53.280 --> 01:12:58.080
showing up, scaling it up, increasing the scope is&nbsp;
much easier once you're already the kind of person&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:58.080 --> 01:13:03.780
who's doing it every day. Yeah, those are good&nbsp;
examples. I think actually maybe, I don't know if&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:03.780 --> 01:13:08.100
it's your question, Tim, I feel like I've heard&nbsp;
you say it before, but this idea of, what would&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:08.100 --> 01:13:12.420
this look like if it was easy? Or, what would&nbsp;
it look like to be the kind of person who looks&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:12.420 --> 01:13:17.100
forward to writing or looks forward to meditating&nbsp;
or looks forward to working out? That's a question&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:17.100 --> 01:13:22.920
worth taking seriously, because you can usually&nbsp;
design the habit to feel like, hey, this isn't a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:22.920 --> 01:13:27.120
chore. This is something, this is how I want to&nbsp;
be spending my days. And that's really where you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:27.120 --> 01:13:32.640
want to get to because for a habit to last, it's&nbsp;
got to be part of your normal lifestyle. It has to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:32.640 --> 01:13:36.360
be something that, like you want to show up and&nbsp;
do this each day. And so you're trying to find&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:36.360 --> 01:13:40.080
the version of it that benefits you the most.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. "What would this look like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:40.080 --> 01:13:45.720
or what might this look like if it were easy?"&nbsp;
is a question I ask myself all the time. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:47.280 --> 01:13:52.260
part of the reason for that is that I've,&nbsp;
for most of my life, prided myself on having&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:53.040 --> 01:13:58.980
high pain tolerance, being able to do the hard&nbsp;
things and the service of the long term. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:58.980 --> 01:14:04.440
when you're starting out, like you said, kind&nbsp;
of clawing your way to any good opportunity or&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:04.440 --> 01:14:12.360
any open window when you're just kind of slipping&nbsp;
back on the mud slope of establishing any type of,&nbsp;

01:14:12.360 --> 01:14:17.160
James Clear: Yeah. You're opening windows that&nbsp;
are closed. People are like, that was locked.&nbsp;

01:14:17.160 --> 01:14:22.560
Tim Ferriss: Exactly. Like clawing like a raccoon&nbsp;
at the window trying to figure out the lock. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:23.640 --> 01:14:31.680
at least for me, I think I began to associate&nbsp;
results with grinding or some degree of effort.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:31.680 --> 01:14:38.820
And so the association would be, if my effort&nbsp;
isn't above a certain threshold, I am not&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:38.820 --> 01:14:46.800
doing the thing that will produce an optimal&nbsp;
result, but past a certain point that can be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:47.460 --> 01:14:54.120
false filter, if that makes any sense. So I&nbsp;
ask myself that constantly. If I'm recording a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:54.120 --> 01:14:57.480
podcast, I've thought, just to give an example,&nbsp;
I haven't done this yet, but I've thought,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:57.480 --> 01:15:03.840
you know what? The technology is good enough. Why&nbsp;
do I do podcasts? At least as it stands right now&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:03.840 --> 01:15:11.460
in a static position. Why not figure out, get the&nbsp;
best headset available, figure out connectivity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:11.460 --> 01:15:18.240
and go for a walk, figure out a way to record a&nbsp;
podcast while I'm walking. I would check off a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:18.240 --> 01:15:23.100
number of these laws that you mentioned.
James Clear: Microphone in one hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:23.100 --> 01:15:30.780
Starlink dish in the other. You're just&nbsp;
going to be like the ultimate version of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:30.780 --> 01:15:35.400
the guys sifting for gold on the beach.
Tim Ferriss: Oh, yeah, that'd be amazing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:35.400 --> 01:15:38.880
I just put the Starlink on the back of&nbsp;
my head, mount it on top of the headset.&nbsp;

01:15:40.080 --> 01:15:42.476
Let's use Atomic Habits as the case study.
James Clear: Yeah.&nbsp;

01:15:42.476 --> 01:15:47.100
Tim Ferriss: How would you deconstruct&nbsp;
Atomic Habits both in its writing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:47.100 --> 01:15:53.040
but then also its launch, right? Because those are&nbsp;
intertwined in what made it successful. What were&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:53.040 --> 01:16:01.200
the things that really seemed to be the Archimedes&nbsp;
levers for producing, to the extent that you can,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:01.200 --> 01:16:03.827
right? You're on one side of the tennis net.
James Clear: Yeah.&nbsp;

01:16:03.827 --> 01:16:06.420
Tim Ferriss: So any examples?
James Clear: This will probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:06.420 --> 01:16:09.960
be a long answer because there's a lot to talk&nbsp;
about. So let's break it into two categories,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:09.960 --> 01:16:17.100
writing and marketing. And also the truth&nbsp;
is, the honest answer might be like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:17.100 --> 01:16:22.860
I don't know, I got lucky. Maybe that's&nbsp;
the truth. But I suspect that there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:22.860 --> 01:16:27.600
things, a lot of things you can do to influence&nbsp;
the outcome. And so I'll walk you through what&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:27.600 --> 01:16:34.260
I tried to do. So on the writing side, I think&nbsp;
you have to operate with this assumption of like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:34.260 --> 01:16:41.580
let me create more value than I'm going to&nbsp;
capture. You try to produce a hit. The ultimate&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:41.580 --> 01:16:47.400
thing that drives book sales is creating something&nbsp;
that is actually genuinely valuable to people. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:47.400 --> 01:16:52.980
it has to be so good that people will talk about&nbsp;
it. Word of mouth, you know, Atomic Habits has&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:52.980 --> 01:16:57.180
sold over 10 million copies at this point.
We could argue, I'd be interested to know&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:57.180 --> 01:17:02.820
what you think the estimate is, Tim, but there's&nbsp;
some number of copies that you could sell just by&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:02.820 --> 01:17:07.500
having an audience and a good marketing plan&nbsp;
and sheer force of will and putting a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:07.500 --> 01:17:12.720
effort in. Maybe it's 25,000, maybe it's&nbsp;
a hundred thousand, I don't know. At some&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:12.720 --> 01:17:17.400
point there's some number where you can't get&nbsp;
beyond that, even if you have a large audience&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:18.120 --> 01:17:23.940
just by the marketing energy. It has to be word&nbsp;
of mouth. And I think certainly once you're in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:23.940 --> 01:17:29.160
millions or tens of millions of copies, the only&nbsp;
way a book grows that much is people recommend it.&nbsp;

01:17:29.160 --> 01:17:35.280
And I like Seth Godin's measure for this, which&nbsp;
is, he says, "If you want word of mouth, you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:35.280 --> 01:17:41.100
to create something remarkable." And that means&nbsp;
that it's worthy of remark, that it's worthy of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:41.100 --> 01:17:46.980
talking about. And so you have to start with that.&nbsp;
So the three things that I always focus on are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:46.980 --> 01:17:51.180
am I writing something that's timeless?&nbsp;
So if it's timeless and evergreen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:51.180 --> 01:17:56.640
then you have a longer period where it could&nbsp;
become a hit. If it's something that's just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:56.640 --> 01:18:01.200
really relevant to the current moment, two years&nbsp;
from now, you've kind of passed your window for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:01.200 --> 01:18:04.080
it taking off. But if it's genuinely timeless —
Tim Ferriss: Like the blog post you wrote that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:04.080 --> 01:18:06.587
got to the producers for television.
James Clear: Yes, right, exactly.&nbsp;

01:18:06.587 --> 01:18:08.640
Tim Ferriss: Similar example.
James Clear: Right.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:08.640 --> 01:18:13.080
Am I writing something that's pretty universal?&nbsp;
So that's another kind of filter for me.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:14.040 --> 01:18:17.760
Everybody has habits. Everybody's trying to&nbsp;
build good habits or break bad ones, even if&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:17.760 --> 01:18:22.260
they're not thinking about it carefully, it's&nbsp;
just kind of part of life. And so not everybody&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:22.260 --> 01:18:26.820
in the world's going to buy the book, but pretty&nbsp;
much anybody can look at the cover and be like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:26.820 --> 01:18:31.140
yeah, I get why that would be useful. That's&nbsp;
a thing that most people are going to want.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:32.280 --> 01:18:37.080
So timeless, is it universal? And then I feel&nbsp;
like you have to pick something that fascinates&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:37.080 --> 01:18:40.980
you because if you're genuinely interested&nbsp;
in it, that comes through in the writing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:42.600 --> 01:18:47.340
I think Morgan Housel has some kind of line&nbsp;
where it's like, "Writing for yourself is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:47.340 --> 01:18:54.420
fun and it shows. Writing for other people is&nbsp;
work and it shows." And people have a good BS&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:54.420 --> 01:19:00.780
meter for that. And you need to pick in your,
Tim Ferriss: Quick side sell for Morgan, The&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:00.780 --> 01:19:07.007
Psychology of Money. The title may seem generic.&nbsp;
The book is outstanding. I really enjoyed it.&nbsp;

01:19:07.007 --> 01:19:11.700
James Clear: Yeah. Yeah, he's fantastic. Okay. So&nbsp;
that's kind of like my write something timeless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:11.700 --> 01:19:15.720
write something universal, write something that&nbsp;
fascinates you and do all that with the intention&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:15.720 --> 01:19:22.620
of creating something genuinely remarkable or that&nbsp;
creates value for people. Those things sound good.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:22.620 --> 01:19:27.900
I think it's worth asking like, what does it&nbsp;
look like in practice to actually do that? So&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:27.900 --> 01:19:30.660
let me just give you some examples.
Tim Ferriss: And I'm just going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:30.660 --> 01:19:34.320
keep interrupting because I've had too much&nbsp;
caffeine. I'm going to blame it on the caffeine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:35.640 --> 01:19:39.360
I would also love to at some point, so you're&nbsp;
going to give some examples. I would love to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:39.360 --> 01:19:49.320
know how you thought about modeling other&nbsp;
books or constructing the format and layout&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:49.320 --> 01:19:53.327
of your book. Not the visual layout per se.
James Clear: No, I know what you mean.&nbsp;

01:19:53.327 --> 01:19:57.300
Tim Ferriss: Structuring the book. Because&nbsp;
I've seen many examples of you deconstructing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:58.320 --> 01:20:03.960
sentences, quotes, application&nbsp;
essays. We probably won't get to that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:03.960 --> 01:20:10.080
but when you went to Switzerland, right?&nbsp;
You're very good at modeling. So at some&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:10.080 --> 01:20:13.740
point I'd love for you to speak to that.
James Clear: Yeah. I think you should&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:13.740 --> 01:20:17.400
deconstruct the cool things that you see in&nbsp;
life. Like when you come across something that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:17.400 --> 01:20:21.060
you really like or that you think is cool, so&nbsp;
in my case, I was writing a book, so how do I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:21.060 --> 01:20:27.000
deconstruct bestsellers? What's going on there?&nbsp;
And if you find a single example of something,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:27.000 --> 01:20:31.860
it doesn't really tell you anything. But&nbsp;
if you start to find patterns, then that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:31.860 --> 01:20:36.960
probably something to pay attention to. So I&nbsp;
think, actually this is a great segue to some&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:36.960 --> 01:20:41.400
of these examples I was going to give. So let's&nbsp;
say when I was writing Atomic Habits early on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:41.400 --> 01:20:46.260
I was like, I know what I'm trying to achieve.&nbsp;
I'm trying to write the most comprehensive&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:46.260 --> 01:20:51.720
and most useful book that's ever been written on&nbsp;
habits. Now I have no idea if I achieved that. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:51.720 --> 01:20:55.740
it's not really for me to decide if we're being&nbsp;
honest, I don't get to choose that it did that.&nbsp;

01:20:55.740 --> 01:21:00.300
That's up to the readers to decide is that a&nbsp;
mark that you hit or not? But I think that has&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:00.300 --> 01:21:04.200
to be the target. You've got to start there.&nbsp;
Otherwise, you're never just going to stumble&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:04.200 --> 01:21:08.760
into that outcome. So you have to try&nbsp;
to intend to create something great.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:10.140 --> 01:21:14.640
So how do we do that? I looked at a bunch of&nbsp;
books, not just about habits, but also other&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:14.640 --> 01:21:18.660
bestsellers. One of the first things I did was&nbsp;
I went to the table of contents for all those&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:18.660 --> 01:21:23.820
books. And then I was like, how are these books&nbsp;
arranged? And what you'll find is that a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:23.820 --> 01:21:30.120
bestselling books, they break things into thirds&nbsp;
or it doesn't always have to be thirds, but they&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:30.120 --> 01:21:36.420
have a clear structure that the book goes through&nbsp;
so that you can look at the table of contents. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:36.420 --> 01:21:40.500
even though you don't know what's in the chapters,&nbsp;
you know exactly where you're headed as a reader.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:40.500 --> 01:21:45.360
What people don't want is they don't want to open&nbsp;
up a book and feel lost, feel like, "I have no&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:45.360 --> 01:21:48.600
idea where this guy's going to go with this." And&nbsp;
it kind of feels like they're wasting their time.&nbsp;

01:21:49.860 --> 01:21:54.960
A couple examples. You could do, and I thought&nbsp;
about these, but I didn't actually use these for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:54.960 --> 01:21:59.760
Atomic Habits. I could've had a table of contents&nbsp;
that was like "Habits of the Past," "Habits of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:59.760 --> 01:22:04.080
Present," "Habits of the Future." You know pretty&nbsp;
clearly where that book's going to go, if you see&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:04.080 --> 01:22:09.840
those categories in the table of contents. Or&nbsp;
Power of Habit is another example. If you look&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:09.840 --> 01:22:15.240
at the table of contents there, I think he does&nbsp;
"Habits of Individuals," "Habits of Businesses,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:15.240 --> 01:22:19.860
and then "Habits of Societies." So it's like&nbsp;
individuals, organizations, societies, and you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:19.860 --> 01:22:25.140
can see how he's kind of moving up the hierarchy&nbsp;
as you go through the book. Another one that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:25.140 --> 01:22:28.500
looked at, I can't remember which one of Cal&nbsp;
Newport's books it was, but it was either Deep&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:28.500 --> 01:22:34.620
Work or something like that. But he had a section&nbsp;
on, here are the rules that you need to follow and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:34.620 --> 01:22:38.760
then there were four rules or six rules, and so&nbsp;
each one of those got their section in the book.&nbsp;

01:22:39.720 --> 01:22:44.640
The point is just there needs to be a clear&nbsp;
roadmap for the reader. And if you start&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:44.640 --> 01:22:48.120
looking through what other bestsellers are&nbsp;
doing, you can start to think about how to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:48.120 --> 01:22:53.520
transport that onto your own experience.
Another thing I did was I tried to look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:53.520 --> 01:22:57.300
what is the average chapter length for a lot&nbsp;
of these books. When I signed the book deal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:58.080 --> 01:23:02.160
the publisher and the editors I was working&nbsp;
with, which I love my publishing team by the way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:02.160 --> 01:23:08.760
but they were kind of encouraging me to write&nbsp;
longer chapters, like 6,000 or 8,000 words. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:08.760 --> 01:23:13.740
I was coming at this as a blogger. Most of the&nbsp;
things I wrote were like 2,000 words or 1,500&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:13.740 --> 01:23:18.780
words. Eventually what I settled on was most of&nbsp;
the chapters were between 2,000 and 3,000 words.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:18.780 --> 01:23:23.700
And that felt like the right one, you know?
Tim Ferriss: And just for folks to have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:23.700 --> 01:23:27.527
reference point. I mean one printed page, what&nbsp;
would you say, it's roughly like 350 words?&nbsp;

01:23:27.527 --> 01:23:31.140
James Clear: Yeah, I think most of&nbsp;
my chapters were about 10 pages. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:32.760 --> 01:23:36.540
300 words. Yeah, if it's a 3,000 word chapter,&nbsp;
maybe it's 10 or 12 pages, something like that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:38.040 --> 01:23:44.400
So yeah, figuring out the length. I started&nbsp;
to notice other things that novels would do or&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:45.180 --> 01:23:50.880
books that weren't necessarily non-fiction. The&nbsp;
chapters are really shortened. There's kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:50.880 --> 01:23:55.020
this momentum that gets into it. It kind of&nbsp;
gets you flipping pages and it's easier to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:55.020 --> 01:23:58.380
keep churning through the chapters. And so I&nbsp;
wanted to have that kind of chapter length.&nbsp;

01:23:59.580 --> 01:24:03.900
I actually think Atomic Habits is still a&nbsp;
little bit longer than I was hoping. I just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:03.900 --> 01:24:09.840
couldn't figure out to compress it more. My&nbsp;
initial manuscript for the book was 700. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:09.840 --> 01:24:15.360
was like 712 pages or something. And the&nbsp;
finished version of the book is like 250.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:15.360 --> 01:24:22.620
So I compressed it by 66 percent or whatever.&nbsp;
So it's much smaller than it started out as,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:23.280 --> 01:24:27.240
which I think is good because I feel like&nbsp;
a lot of books, especially business books,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:27.240 --> 01:24:31.740
they could have been a blog post or a 20-page&nbsp;
paper and they get turned into a 200-page book.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:31.740 --> 01:24:34.080
So I wanted to do the opposite.
Tim Ferriss: I fucking hate&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:34.080 --> 01:24:37.680
that. It's the worst. It's so bad.
James Clear: But I actually think&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:37.680 --> 01:24:41.940
if you look at books that sell really well, a&nbsp;
lot of the time they're like 180 to 220 pages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:41.940 --> 01:24:47.340
they're actually even a little bit shorter than&nbsp;
what I ended up hitting. So these are all examples&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:47.340 --> 01:24:52.680
of deconstructing bestsellers on what's going&nbsp;
on. Probably the most important place to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:52.680 --> 01:24:59.160
this is with titles. So I have a spreadsheet of&nbsp;
hundreds of titles for books. The way that I did&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:59.160 --> 01:25:02.760
it was I said, "I'm only going to look at books&nbsp;
that have sold a million copies or more. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:02.760 --> 01:25:05.880
what I'm trying to achieve. So let me just see&nbsp;
how many books I can find that have done that."&nbsp;

01:25:06.480 --> 01:25:10.380
Tim Ferriss: Quick question, tactical&nbsp;
question, did you do that through&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:10.980 --> 01:25:17.340
access to Nielsen BookScan?
James Clear: I didn't have access&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:17.340 --> 01:25:22.020
to BookScan because I was just a poor&nbsp;
blogger who was trying to make a book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:22.020 --> 01:25:27.300
so I didn't have access to any of that stuff. But&nbsp;
what I did was I just did tons of Google searches.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:28.380 --> 01:25:32.936
Usually what happens is that when an author hits a&nbsp;
million copies or four million or whatever, they —&nbsp;

01:25:32.936 --> 01:25:35.580
Tim Ferriss: They'll let you know.
James Clear: Yeah, they let you know. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:35.580 --> 01:25:40.440
so there's all kinds of stuff, or you'll find like&nbsp;
if you just search a title and then copies sold,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:41.100 --> 01:25:44.880
there's New York Times articles that&nbsp;
profile an author and they say, "Their&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:44.880 --> 01:25:48.840
book sold six million copies," or whatever.&nbsp;
So it was just a bunch of legwork like that.&nbsp;

01:25:49.440 --> 01:25:53.460
Anyway, I put together that spreadsheet.&nbsp;
Let's say I've got 150 titles or something,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:53.460 --> 01:26:00.240
and then I started to look for patterns. So&nbsp;
what format do all these titles follow? I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:00.240 --> 01:26:03.780
think I ended up coming up with, they're about&nbsp;
six. I won't be able to remember them all off&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:03.780 --> 01:26:09.540
the top of my head, but the most common format&nbsp;
is the blank of blank. So The Power of Habit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:09.540 --> 01:26:14.580
The Life-Changing Magic of Tidying Up, The&nbsp;
Power of Now, The Power of Positive Thinking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:15.360 --> 01:26:19.260
The Subtle Art of Not Giving a Fuck, The blah,&nbsp;
blah, blah. So it's always the blank of blank,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:21.660 --> 01:26:28.080
The War of Art. It's a very proven format. The&nbsp;
Psychology of Money, we can just keep going on and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:28.080 --> 01:26:34.140
on and on. There are so many examples like that.
Usually what people do is they take their topic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:34.140 --> 01:26:38.760
and that's the second piece, and then they&nbsp;
take some type of descriptor, and that's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:38.760 --> 01:26:48.600
first one. Ideally, you're combining things that&nbsp;
are not usually combined like, The Life-Changing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:48.600 --> 01:26:53.040
Magic of Tidying Up. So tidying up is the&nbsp;
topic of the book, but now you're telling&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:53.040 --> 01:26:57.120
me that it's life-changing. And what you find&nbsp;
is a lot of these bestselling books, they have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:57.120 --> 01:27:03.660
some element of contrast in the title. There's&nbsp;
something that's unexpected about it. Another&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:03.660 --> 01:27:07.260
common format is you just take, and this is —
Tim Ferriss: Subtle Art of Not Giving a Fuck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:07.260 --> 01:27:10.320
right? You don't usually think subtle art.
