I'll get to Atomic Habits in a second, but&nbsp;
Extreme Ownership by Jocko. The idea is you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:32.160 --> 01:27:35.880
take ownership and responsibility for your&nbsp;
life, but you do it in this extreme way. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:35.880 --> 01:27:41.220
not just a regular idea of taking ownership.&nbsp;
Or Cal Newport, Deep Work. You're doing work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:41.220 --> 01:27:44.460
but it's a special kind of work. It's not the&nbsp;
normal type of thing that you're thinking about.&nbsp;

01:27:47.700 --> 01:27:52.560
These are just examples. There are a couple more&nbsp;
patterns that I found. And then ultimately you've&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:52.560 --> 01:27:56.220
got to decide like, which one makes the most&nbsp;
sense for me? Which one feels like it's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:56.220 --> 01:28:00.000
best fit for my book?
Tim Ferriss:&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:00.000 --> 01:28:04.980
And which one can you live with forever?
James Clear: Yes. Titles are really tricky&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:04.980 --> 01:28:11.160
because they need to pass a lot of filters. They&nbsp;
need to cover what the book is actually about.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:11.940 --> 01:28:15.900
Like in my case, it needed to actually talk&nbsp;
about habits. I think it's important to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:15.900 --> 01:28:22.740
some element of contrast. In my case, "atomic"&nbsp;
has multiple meanings. It can be tiny or small,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:22.740 --> 01:28:29.100
so your habit should be easy to do. It can be the&nbsp;
fundamental unit in a larger system. So like atoms&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:29.100 --> 01:28:34.620
build into molecules and systems versus goals is&nbsp;
a big part of the book. It can be the source of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:34.620 --> 01:28:41.340
immense energy or power. So I think if you combine&nbsp;
all those three meanings, you get the big arc of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:41.340 --> 01:28:46.380
the book, which is you make changes that are small&nbsp;
and easy to do, you layer them on top of each&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:46.380 --> 01:28:51.420
other like units in a larger system, and then you&nbsp;
end up with these powerful or remarkable results&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:51.960 --> 01:28:57.120
as a byproduct. So for all of those reasons, I&nbsp;
felt like atomic was a great word to describe it.&nbsp;

01:28:59.700 --> 01:29:04.080
So It needs to describe what the topic is.&nbsp;
It needs to be interesting and compelling or&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:04.080 --> 01:29:10.500
a little bit different. Sometimes good habits&nbsp;
are a little bit weird in a sense. Nobody was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:10.500 --> 01:29:15.000
really using the phrase "atomic habits"&nbsp;
before the book came out. It wasn't like&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:15.000 --> 01:29:19.200
a way that you would describe a habit. You might&nbsp;
describe it as small, but you wouldn't describe&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:19.200 --> 01:29:20.640
it as atomic. And so that allows me to —
Tim Ferriss: Good habits also, if it had&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:20.640 --> 01:29:24.900
been good habits, just for people out&nbsp;
there, you're going to be competing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:24.900 --> 01:29:30.120
with the entire internet for search results.
James Clear: I actually think a lot of titles fall&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:30.120 --> 01:29:35.640
into this pitfall, which is they use a phrase that&nbsp;
is commonly used and you want a phrase that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:35.640 --> 01:29:42.600
can own in the reader's mind. You don't want to be&nbsp;
competing with all the other things that use the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:42.600 --> 01:29:48.240
phrase "good habits." 4-Hour Workweek is a good&nbsp;
example of this. Nobody really used that phrase&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:48.240 --> 01:29:53.760
before the book. It was a little bit weird in the&nbsp;
sense that it wasn't part of normal conversation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:53.760 --> 01:29:55.666
and that allowed you to own it.
Tim Ferriss: For sure.&nbsp;

01:29:55.666 --> 01:30:00.240
James Clear: It also had that element of contrast&nbsp;
where you're like, "Man, I work for 40 hours a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:00.240 --> 01:30:04.440
week, you're telling me I can work for four?"&nbsp;
So it's this surprising thing. Another example&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:04.440 --> 01:30:10.500
that might be like Rich Dad Poor Dad. That book&nbsp;
has this element of contrast between the two.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:11.220 --> 01:30:16.680
So it needs to be something that you actually&nbsp;
want, it needs to cover the topic of the book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:16.680 --> 01:30:20.160
it needs to have this element of contrast.&nbsp;
There's a lot of filters for it to pass,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:20.160 --> 01:30:23.580
and that's why coming up with good titles is hard.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. Let me add one thing too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:23.580 --> 01:30:28.440
because there may be some — I have a lot of&nbsp;
engineers and computer scientists and so on who&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:28.440 --> 01:30:33.240
listen to this podcast. If you guys are starting&nbsp;
to develop hives, because this sounds like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

